2. Systematic Literature Review and Research‑Gap Analysis

A systematic literature review was conducted in accordance with the PRISMA 2020 guidelines, drawing on peer‑reviewed sources from Scopus, IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, and MDPI. The review encompassed four thematic clusters:

· UAV‑based 3D reconstruction and digital twins:: The most comprehensive 2025 systematic review of UAV‑based digital twins, across 145 publications, concluded that coordinating and controlling heterogeneous multi‑UAV systems for structural health monitoring remains a significant unsolved challenge, with the overwhelming majority of studies limited to single‑UAV or homogeneous swarms inspecting bridges, tanks, or wind turbines.
· Robotic aircraft skin inspection:: A 2025 PRISMA‑guided review of 73 publications on robotic aircraft skin inspection identified that multi‑robot heterogeneous collaborative systems, intelligent dynamic task scheduling, and large‑model‑based airworthiness assessment are all open research gaps, with no mature integrated system yet validated in an operational setting.
· AI‑enabled NDT in aerospace:: A 2025 comprehensive review of AI for image‑ and signal‑based NDT in aerospace structures highlighted the critical need for explainable and physics‑informed architectures, uncertainty quantification, and digital‑twin‑enabled deployment as prerequisites for certifiable AI‑based NDT.
· Heterogeneous swarm inspection:: The SOAR system demonstrated a heterogeneous drone swarm for 3D reconstruction using a LiDAR‑equipped explorer and multiple photographer drones, achieving simultaneous exploration and photography, but SOAR was limited to exterior visual reconstruction alone and did not include contact NDT, thermal mapping, or internal confined‑space inspection. The FailProof project (KU Leuven, 2024‑ongoing) targets autonomous failure prognosis using unmanned vehicles, but it has not addressed the combination of five distinct NDT modalities on a cryogenic launch‑vehicle hull.

Gap‑analysis conclusion: No study has integrated (i) heterogeneous multi‑modal NDT (LiDAR, thermal, micro‑optical, ultrasonic, eddy‑current, confined‑space LiDAR‑SLAM), (ii) a physics‑informed prognostic digital twin, (iii) a knowledge‑graph‑grounded LLM safety‑reasoning layer, and (iv) a 6G semantic communication architecture onto a single reusable launch vehicle. This PhD thus occupies a uniquely unfilled intersection of swarm robotics, non‑destructive evaluation, physics‑informed machine learning, and safety‑critical AI.

