B.10 Launch‑Pad Electromagnetic and Acoustic Environment: Simplified Physics

The launch pad during propellant loading creates an acoustic field with sound pressure levels up to 160 dB (re 20 µPa) near the vehicle, dominated by low‑frequency noise from venting and turbopump spin‑up. The drone’s vibration‑isolation rail is tuned to attenuate frequencies above 50 Hz by ≥20 dB, verified through a single‑degree‑of‑freedom transmissibility model:

T(\omega) = \sqrt{ \frac{1 + (2\zeta \omega/\omega_n)^2}{(1 - (\omega/\omega_n)^2)^2 + (2\zeta \omega/\omega_n)^2} }

with natural frequency \omega_n = 8 Hz and damping \zeta = 0.07, providing attenuation at the dominant drone‑vibration frequencies (100–500 Hz).

For RF multipath off the stainless‑steel hull, we model the channel as a Ricean fading model with K-factor estimated from the ratio of direct to reflected path lengths. Spread‑spectrum UWB (3.1–10.6 GHz) provides processing gain G_p = 10 \log_{10}(B_{\text{UWB}} / R_b) \approx 23 dB for a 1 Mbps data rate, sufficient to maintain reliable communication at 10 m standoff even with multipath.

